1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,462
Most of us remember the early sixties

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:03,726
in black and white.

3
00:00:03,906 --> 00:00:06,066
The old photographs in the family album.

4
00:00:06,246 --> 00:00:07,453
The news footage.

5
00:00:07,633 --> 00:00:09,084
The television shows.

6
00:00:09,264 --> 00:00:10,669
But that's not how it looked.

7
00:00:10,849 --> 00:00:12,138
The sky was blue.

8
00:00:12,318 --> 00:00:14,785
The cars were turquoise and coral and

9
00:00:14,785 --> 00:00:15,186
cream.

10
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:17,630
The aprons had yellow flowers on them.

11
00:00:17,810 --> 00:00:20,469
Today, let's see it the way it

12
00:00:20,469 --> 00:00:21,443
really was.

13
00:00:21,623 --> 00:00:22,517
In color.

14
00:00:22,867 --> 00:00:25,381
We're going to walk through one small

15
00:00:25,381 --> 00:00:27,691
American town, from one end to the

16
00:00:27,691 --> 00:00:30,205
other, in the early nineteen sixties.

17
00:00:30,385 --> 00:00:31,488
The main street.

18
00:00:31,668 --> 00:00:32,399
The diner.

19
00:00:32,579 --> 00:00:33,346
The school.

20
00:00:33,526 --> 00:00:34,303
The church.

21
00:00:34,483 --> 00:00:35,749
The barber shop.

22
00:00:35,929 --> 00:00:38,263
The houses with the porch lights on

23
00:00:38,263 --> 00:00:38,797
at dusk.

24
00:00:38,977 --> 00:00:40,530
If you grew up in a town

25
00:00:40,530 --> 00:00:42,796
like this, you're about to go home.

26
00:00:43,146 --> 00:00:45,650
This town could be in Ohio,

27
00:00:45,650 --> 00:00:48,339
or Kansas, or North Carolina,

28
00:00:48,339 --> 00:00:50,194
or upstate New York.

29
00:00:50,374 --> 00:00:51,890
In nineteen sixty-two,

30
00:00:51,890 --> 00:00:54,784
about thirty percent of Americans lived in

31
00:00:54,784 --> 00:00:58,230
places with fewer than twenty-five hundred people,

32
00:00:58,230 --> 00:01:00,642
and millions more in towns not much

33
00:01:00,642 --> 00:01:01,125
bigger.

34
00:01:01,305 --> 00:01:03,000
They all looked a little different.

35
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:05,832
And somehow, they all looked exactly the

36
00:01:05,832 --> 00:01:06,163
same.

37
00:01:06,513 --> 00:01:09,079
Main Street was the heart of everything.

38
00:01:09,259 --> 00:01:11,563
On one block you'd find the bank,

39
00:01:11,563 --> 00:01:12,889
the hardware store,

40
00:01:12,889 --> 00:01:15,192
the five-and-dime, the drugstore,

41
00:01:15,192 --> 00:01:16,239
the shoe store,

42
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:17,495
the movie theater,

43
00:01:17,495 --> 00:01:20,357
and a department store with mannequins in

44
00:01:20,357 --> 00:01:21,194
the windows.

45
00:01:21,374 --> 00:01:23,743
Every building had a name painted on

46
00:01:23,743 --> 00:01:26,177
the brick, and every name belonged to

47
00:01:26,177 --> 00:01:27,295
a family in town.

48
00:01:27,645 --> 00:01:29,503
Saturday was the big day.

49
00:01:29,683 --> 00:01:32,590
Farm families drove in from the countryside

50
00:01:32,590 --> 00:01:33,131
to shop.

51
00:01:33,311 --> 00:01:35,204
The sidewalks filled up.

52
00:01:35,384 --> 00:01:37,583
Mothers went from store to store with

53
00:01:37,583 --> 00:01:38,890
lists in their purses,

54
00:01:38,890 --> 00:01:41,625
while fathers stood outside the hardware store

55
00:01:41,625 --> 00:01:43,170
talking about the weather,

56
00:01:43,170 --> 00:01:44,834
the crops and the ball game.

57
00:01:45,184 --> 00:01:47,952
The five-and-dime was where kids spent their

58
00:01:47,952 --> 00:01:48,644
allowances.

59
00:01:48,824 --> 00:01:51,448
Candy by the pound from glass cases.

60
00:01:51,628 --> 00:01:53,706
Comic books for twelve cents.

61
00:01:53,886 --> 00:01:55,046
Toy cap guns,

62
00:01:55,046 --> 00:01:58,259
jacks, marbles, model airplane kits,

63
00:01:58,259 --> 00:02:00,847
and goldfish in a tank at the

64
00:02:00,847 --> 00:02:01,293
back.

65
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:04,223
The wooden floors creaked under your feet,

66
00:02:04,223 --> 00:02:06,711
and the whole store smelled of popcorn

67
00:02:06,711 --> 00:02:07,824
and floor polish.

68
00:02:08,175 --> 00:02:09,418
At the drugstore,

69
00:02:09,418 --> 00:02:11,832
the soda fountain was still open.

70
00:02:12,012 --> 00:02:14,118
You sat on a spinning stool at

71
00:02:14,118 --> 00:02:15,451
the marble counter,

72
00:02:15,451 --> 00:02:17,417
and the soda jerk made you a

73
00:02:17,417 --> 00:02:20,155
cherry phosphate or a chocolate malt in

74
00:02:20,155 --> 00:02:21,067
a tall glass.

75
00:02:21,247 --> 00:02:24,643
The pharmacist knew every family's prescriptions by

76
00:02:24,643 --> 00:02:27,439
heart, and he'd often deliver them himself

77
00:02:27,439 --> 00:02:28,504
on the way home.

78
00:02:28,854 --> 00:02:30,257
Parking was diagonal,

79
00:02:30,257 --> 00:02:32,195
right in front of the stores,

80
00:02:32,195 --> 00:02:34,333
and a nickel in the meter bought

81
00:02:34,333 --> 00:02:34,867
an hour.

82
00:02:35,047 --> 00:02:37,152
The cars along the curb were a

83
00:02:37,152 --> 00:02:38,275
parade of color.

84
00:02:38,455 --> 00:02:41,810
Two-tone Chevrolets in turquoise and white.

85
00:02:41,990 --> 00:02:43,546
Pink Cadillacs.

86
00:02:43,726 --> 00:02:46,542
Station wagons with fake wood on the

87
00:02:46,542 --> 00:02:47,012
sides.

88
00:02:47,192 --> 00:02:50,396
Pickup trucks, dusty from the farm roads.

89
00:02:50,746 --> 00:02:52,102
Above the stores,

90
00:02:52,102 --> 00:02:53,697
on the second floor,

91
00:02:53,697 --> 00:02:55,053
were the offices.

92
00:02:55,233 --> 00:02:56,069
The doctor.

93
00:02:56,249 --> 00:02:57,085
The dentist.

94
00:02:57,265 --> 00:02:57,961
The lawyer.

95
00:02:58,141 --> 00:02:59,813
The insurance man.

96
00:02:59,993 --> 00:03:02,591
You climbed a narrow staircase with a

97
00:03:02,591 --> 00:03:04,979
brass railing and a door with gold

98
00:03:04,979 --> 00:03:07,296
letters painted on frosted glass.

99
00:03:07,476 --> 00:03:10,149
The dentist's drill could be heard from

100
00:03:10,149 --> 00:03:12,480
the sidewalk on a quiet afternoon.

101
00:03:12,830 --> 00:03:14,794
Down the street was the diner,

102
00:03:14,794 --> 00:03:16,954
a long room with booths along the

103
00:03:16,954 --> 00:03:19,180
windows and a counter with stools.

104
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,488
The waitress called everybody honey and refilled

105
00:03:22,488 --> 00:03:24,248
your coffee without asking.

106
00:03:24,428 --> 00:03:25,826
Breakfast was eggs,

107
00:03:25,826 --> 00:03:28,549
bacon, toast and hash browns for well

108
00:03:28,549 --> 00:03:29,652
under a dollar.

109
00:03:29,832 --> 00:03:32,300
The pie case by the register held

110
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:34,767
lemon meringue, apple and cherry.

111
00:03:35,117 --> 00:03:37,405
The same men sat at the same

112
00:03:37,405 --> 00:03:39,122
stools every morning.

113
00:03:39,302 --> 00:03:41,263
Farmers, mail carriers,

114
00:03:41,263 --> 00:03:42,712
the police chief,

115
00:03:42,712 --> 00:03:45,525
the man who owned the feed store.

116
00:03:45,705 --> 00:03:48,230
They knew everything that happened in town

117
00:03:48,230 --> 00:03:49,734
before the newspaper did.

118
00:03:49,914 --> 00:03:51,701
If you wanted to know who was

119
00:03:51,701 --> 00:03:53,119
sick, who was courting,

120
00:03:53,119 --> 00:03:54,722
or whose barn burned down,

121
00:03:54,722 --> 00:03:56,078
you went to the diner.

122
00:03:56,429 --> 00:03:58,798
Next door was the barber shop,

123
00:03:58,798 --> 00:03:59,824
with its red,

124
00:03:59,824 --> 00:04:03,220
white and blue pole turning slowly outside.

125
00:04:03,400 --> 00:04:05,172
Inside, three chairs,

126
00:04:05,172 --> 00:04:06,944
a row of men waiting,

127
00:04:06,944 --> 00:04:08,716
a stack of magazines,

128
00:04:08,716 --> 00:04:11,163
and a radio tuned to the ball

129
00:04:11,163 --> 00:04:11,585
game.

130
00:04:11,765 --> 00:04:14,249
A haircut cost around a dollar and

131
00:04:14,249 --> 00:04:14,761
a half.

132
00:04:14,941 --> 00:04:17,004
Little boys sat on a board laid

133
00:04:17,004 --> 00:04:18,935
across the arms of the chair.

134
00:04:19,285 --> 00:04:21,270
Most boys got the same haircut.

135
00:04:21,450 --> 00:04:23,030
Short on the sides,

136
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:24,944
a little longer on top,

137
00:04:24,944 --> 00:04:27,522
maybe a flat-top held up with a

138
00:04:27,522 --> 00:04:29,020
pink stick of wax.

139
00:04:29,200 --> 00:04:31,939
Nobody asked what style you wanted.

140
00:04:32,119 --> 00:04:33,575
The barber looked at you,

141
00:04:33,575 --> 00:04:34,856
looked at your father,

142
00:04:34,856 --> 00:04:35,846
and went to work.

143
00:04:36,026 --> 00:04:38,627
And afterward, he handed you a lollipop.

144
00:04:38,977 --> 00:04:41,605
Across from the barber was the post

145
00:04:41,605 --> 00:04:44,234
office, with its flag out front and

146
00:04:44,234 --> 00:04:46,262
its brass mailboxes inside,

147
00:04:46,262 --> 00:04:48,590
each with a little window and a

148
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:49,867
combination dial.

149
00:04:50,047 --> 00:04:52,959
The postmaster knew every family in town

150
00:04:52,959 --> 00:04:55,434
by name, and often by handwriting.

151
00:04:55,614 --> 00:04:57,906
Letters from sons in the army were

152
00:04:57,906 --> 00:05:00,468
handed across the counter with a smile

153
00:05:00,468 --> 00:05:02,558
and a question about how he was

154
00:05:02,558 --> 00:05:02,963
doing.

155
00:05:03,313 --> 00:05:05,237
At the edge of town stood the

156
00:05:05,237 --> 00:05:07,826
school, often a big brick building with

157
00:05:07,826 --> 00:05:09,949
tall windows and the year it was

158
00:05:09,949 --> 00:05:11,741
built carved over the door.

159
00:05:11,921 --> 00:05:13,344
In smaller towns,

160
00:05:13,344 --> 00:05:16,356
all twelve grades might be under one

161
00:05:16,356 --> 00:05:16,774
roof.

162
00:05:16,954 --> 00:05:19,224
The same teachers taught the children of

163
00:05:19,224 --> 00:05:21,550
the children they had taught twenty years

164
00:05:21,550 --> 00:05:21,947
before.

165
00:05:22,297 --> 00:05:25,623
The classrooms had wooden desks with inkwell

166
00:05:25,623 --> 00:05:27,588
holes nobody used anymore,

167
00:05:27,588 --> 00:05:29,931
a clock on the wall that ticked

168
00:05:29,931 --> 00:05:31,821
loudly in the afternoons,

169
00:05:31,821 --> 00:05:34,920
and a portrait of George Washington above

170
00:05:34,920 --> 00:05:36,054
the blackboard.

171
00:05:36,234 --> 00:05:38,832
Every morning began with the Pledge of

172
00:05:38,832 --> 00:05:40,883
Allegiance, hands over hearts,

173
00:05:40,883 --> 00:05:42,933
facing the flag in the corner.

174
00:05:43,284 --> 00:05:46,423
At lunch, the cafeteria served food on

175
00:05:46,423 --> 00:05:47,579
divided trays.

176
00:05:47,759 --> 00:05:50,846
Sloppy joes, fish sticks on Friday,

177
00:05:50,846 --> 00:05:54,198
a square of yellow cake with chocolate

178
00:05:54,198 --> 00:05:54,992
frosting.

179
00:05:55,172 --> 00:05:57,891
Milk came in small glass bottles or

180
00:05:57,891 --> 00:06:00,455
the new little cardboard cartons.

181
00:06:00,635 --> 00:06:03,151
At recess, the girls jumped rope and

182
00:06:03,151 --> 00:06:05,598
the boys played baseball on a dusty

183
00:06:05,598 --> 00:06:07,415
field behind the building.

184
00:06:07,765 --> 00:06:10,884
After school, the big yellow buses lined

185
00:06:10,884 --> 00:06:11,898
up out front.

186
00:06:12,078 --> 00:06:13,619
Town kids walked home,

187
00:06:13,619 --> 00:06:16,211
stopping at the drugstore on the way.

188
00:06:16,391 --> 00:06:18,489
Farm kids rode the bus for an

189
00:06:18,489 --> 00:06:21,167
hour, past the grain elevator and the

190
00:06:21,167 --> 00:06:23,917
water tower, and then went straight to

191
00:06:23,917 --> 00:06:26,956
chores, feeding the animals before supper.

192
00:06:27,306 --> 00:06:29,556
Friday nights in the fall belonged to

193
00:06:29,556 --> 00:06:31,381
the high school football team.

194
00:06:31,561 --> 00:06:33,670
The whole town turned out under the

195
00:06:33,670 --> 00:06:34,092
lights.

196
00:06:34,272 --> 00:06:36,826
The band marched at halftime in uniforms

197
00:06:36,826 --> 00:06:37,847
with gold braid.

198
00:06:38,027 --> 00:06:41,282
The cheerleaders wore saddle shoes and pleated

199
00:06:41,282 --> 00:06:41,778
skirts.

200
00:06:41,958 --> 00:06:43,341
And on Monday morning,

201
00:06:43,341 --> 00:06:45,668
the game was all anybody talked about

202
00:06:45,668 --> 00:06:46,485
at the diner.

203
00:06:46,836 --> 00:06:49,112
The houses in town sat on wide

204
00:06:49,112 --> 00:06:51,085
lots with big shade trees.

205
00:06:51,265 --> 00:06:54,156
White frame houses with green shutters.

206
00:06:54,336 --> 00:06:56,716
Brick bungalows with front porches.

207
00:06:56,896 --> 00:06:59,535
Newer ranch houses on the edge of

208
00:06:59,535 --> 00:07:02,334
town, in pale yellow or mint green,

209
00:07:02,334 --> 00:07:04,894
each with a picture window and a

210
00:07:04,894 --> 00:07:05,534
carport.

211
00:07:05,714 --> 00:07:07,650
Nobody locked the front door,

212
00:07:07,650 --> 00:07:09,719
and the keys stayed in the car.

213
00:07:10,069 --> 00:07:12,106
The porch was where life happened in

214
00:07:12,106 --> 00:07:12,786
the evening.

215
00:07:12,966 --> 00:07:14,687
You sat in a glider or on

216
00:07:14,687 --> 00:07:16,270
the steps after supper,

217
00:07:16,270 --> 00:07:18,887
and people walking by stopped to talk.

218
00:07:19,067 --> 00:07:21,909
The radio played softly through the screen

219
00:07:21,909 --> 00:07:22,248
door.

220
00:07:22,428 --> 00:07:24,831
Fireflies came out over the lawn.

221
00:07:25,011 --> 00:07:26,250
And down the street,

222
00:07:26,250 --> 00:07:28,355
a mother called her children home.

223
00:07:28,705 --> 00:07:31,611
Inside, the kitchens were full of color

224
00:07:31,611 --> 00:07:31,909
too.

225
00:07:32,089 --> 00:07:34,562
Pink or turquoise appliances.

226
00:07:34,742 --> 00:07:37,029
Yellow Formica countertops.

227
00:07:37,209 --> 00:07:39,744
A chrome dinette set with red vinyl

228
00:07:39,744 --> 00:07:40,251
chairs.

229
00:07:40,431 --> 00:07:43,840
Curtains with roosters or teapots printed on

230
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:44,228
them.

231
00:07:44,408 --> 00:07:46,370
A clock shaped like a sunburst.

232
00:07:46,550 --> 00:07:49,977
Women's magazines told homemakers that color made

233
00:07:49,977 --> 00:07:50,886
a happy home,

234
00:07:50,886 --> 00:07:52,355
and they believed it.

235
00:07:52,705 --> 00:07:55,561
Monday was wash day all over town.

236
00:07:55,741 --> 00:07:58,499
Sheets and shirts flapped on clotheslines in

237
00:07:58,499 --> 00:08:01,070
every backyard, and the women talked over

238
00:08:01,070 --> 00:08:03,264
the fences while they hung them up.

239
00:08:03,444 --> 00:08:05,677
A lot of families now owned an

240
00:08:05,677 --> 00:08:07,537
electric washing machine,

241
00:08:07,537 --> 00:08:10,811
but almost everybody still dried the laundry

242
00:08:10,811 --> 00:08:12,895
outside in the sun and wind.

243
00:08:13,245 --> 00:08:15,080
Out back there was a garden,

244
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:16,784
a swing set made of pipes,

245
00:08:16,784 --> 00:08:19,013
and maybe a barbecue grill made of

246
00:08:19,013 --> 00:08:21,569
bricks that a father had built himself.

247
00:08:21,749 --> 00:08:23,111
On summer weekends,

248
00:08:23,111 --> 00:08:26,266
the smell of charcoal and hamburgers drifted

249
00:08:26,266 --> 00:08:27,485
over every fence.

250
00:08:27,665 --> 00:08:29,955
Neighbors came by with a dish of

251
00:08:29,955 --> 00:08:32,459
potato salad and stayed until dark.

252
00:08:32,810 --> 00:08:34,130
On Sunday morning,

253
00:08:34,130 --> 00:08:36,769
the church bells rang all over town.

254
00:08:36,949 --> 00:08:39,580
Families walked or drove to church in

255
00:08:39,580 --> 00:08:40,931
their best clothes.

256
00:08:41,111 --> 00:08:43,607
The women wore hats and gloves.

257
00:08:43,787 --> 00:08:46,394
The little girls wore patent leather shoes

258
00:08:46,394 --> 00:08:47,201
that pinched.

259
00:08:47,381 --> 00:08:49,460
The little boys wore clip-on ties and

260
00:08:49,460 --> 00:08:51,595
tried not to fidget during the sermon.

261
00:08:51,945 --> 00:08:54,190
After church came Sunday dinner,

262
00:08:54,190 --> 00:08:56,225
the biggest meal of the week,

263
00:08:56,225 --> 00:08:58,400
often in the middle of the day.

264
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:00,993
Roast beef or fried chicken,

265
00:09:00,993 --> 00:09:04,871
mashed potatoes, green beans from the garden,

266
00:09:04,871 --> 00:09:06,939
homemade rolls, and pie.

267
00:09:07,119 --> 00:09:10,592
Grandparents, aunts, uncles and cousins crowded around

268
00:09:10,592 --> 00:09:12,779
the table, and the children ate in

269
00:09:12,779 --> 00:09:13,551
the kitchen.

270
00:09:13,901 --> 00:09:14,904
In the summer,

271
00:09:14,904 --> 00:09:17,483
the whole town went to the municipal

272
00:09:17,483 --> 00:09:20,564
swimming pool, which opened on Memorial Day

273
00:09:20,564 --> 00:09:22,283
and closed on Labor Day.

274
00:09:22,463 --> 00:09:24,507
The water smelled of chlorine.

275
00:09:24,687 --> 00:09:26,231
The concrete burned your feet.

276
00:09:26,411 --> 00:09:28,360
And the high dive was the test

277
00:09:28,360 --> 00:09:31,090
of courage for every ten-year-old in town.

278
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:33,848
On summer evenings there was Little League

279
00:09:33,848 --> 00:09:34,880
at the ball field,

280
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:37,001
with the parents in the bleachers and

281
00:09:37,001 --> 00:09:38,894
the coach chewing on a toothpick.

282
00:09:39,074 --> 00:09:41,211
On the Fourth of July there was

283
00:09:41,211 --> 00:09:43,003
a parade down Main Street,

284
00:09:43,003 --> 00:09:44,451
a picnic in the park,

285
00:09:44,451 --> 00:09:46,864
and fireworks over the water tower.

286
00:09:47,044 --> 00:09:48,274
Everybody came.

287
00:09:48,454 --> 00:09:49,302
Everybody.

288
00:09:49,652 --> 00:09:51,251
And in the late summer came the

289
00:09:51,251 --> 00:09:51,869
county fair.

290
00:09:52,049 --> 00:09:55,649
Prize pigs, prize pies and prize quilts.

291
00:09:55,829 --> 00:09:58,522
A Ferris wheel and a merry-go-round.

292
00:09:58,702 --> 00:10:02,661
Cotton candy, caramel apples and corn dogs.

293
00:10:02,841 --> 00:10:05,636
Teenagers walked the midway holding hands for

294
00:10:05,636 --> 00:10:06,568
the first time.

295
00:10:06,748 --> 00:10:07,852
For a lot of kids,

296
00:10:07,852 --> 00:10:09,509
it was the best week of the

297
00:10:09,509 --> 00:10:10,184
whole year.

298
00:10:10,534 --> 00:10:11,995
And on the edge of town,

299
00:10:11,995 --> 00:10:14,307
there was always the drive-in theater.

300
00:10:14,487 --> 00:10:17,681
A giant white screen standing alone in

301
00:10:17,681 --> 00:10:18,353
a field.

302
00:10:18,533 --> 00:10:20,484
A speaker that hung on your car

303
00:10:20,484 --> 00:10:20,925
window.

304
00:10:21,105 --> 00:10:23,854
Children in pajamas in the back seat,

305
00:10:23,854 --> 00:10:26,306
asleep before the second feature.

306
00:10:26,486 --> 00:10:28,223
A carload of teenagers,

307
00:10:28,223 --> 00:10:30,338
one or two of them hiding in

308
00:10:30,338 --> 00:10:32,604
the trunk to save the price of

309
00:10:32,604 --> 00:10:33,359
admission.

310
00:10:33,709 --> 00:10:35,300
As the sun went down,

311
00:10:35,300 --> 00:10:37,799
the neon signs came on along Main

312
00:10:37,799 --> 00:10:38,330
Street.

313
00:10:38,510 --> 00:10:40,774
The red letters over the movie theater.

314
00:10:40,954 --> 00:10:43,659
The green glow of the drugstore sign.

315
00:10:43,839 --> 00:10:47,248
The theater marquee announced the Saturday picture,

316
00:10:47,248 --> 00:10:49,989
and teenagers lined up outside with their

317
00:10:49,989 --> 00:10:52,395
dates, pretending not to be nervous.

318
00:10:52,745 --> 00:10:55,343
Teenagers cruised the main street in their

319
00:10:55,343 --> 00:10:58,066
fathers' cars, turning around in the parking

320
00:10:58,066 --> 00:10:59,922
lot of the drive-in at one end

321
00:10:59,922 --> 00:11:01,963
and the gas station at the other.

322
00:11:02,143 --> 00:11:03,409
Back and forth.

323
00:11:03,589 --> 00:11:05,017
Back and forth.

324
00:11:05,197 --> 00:11:07,435
The radios were tuned to the same

325
00:11:07,435 --> 00:11:09,810
station, so the same song seemed to

326
00:11:09,810 --> 00:11:12,116
be playing from every car at once.

327
00:11:12,466 --> 00:11:13,549
By ten o'clock,

328
00:11:13,549 --> 00:11:15,426
most of the town was dark.

329
00:11:15,606 --> 00:11:17,081
The stores were closed.

330
00:11:17,261 --> 00:11:19,750
A police car drove slowly down Main

331
00:11:19,750 --> 00:11:22,738
Street, the officer checking that the shop

332
00:11:22,738 --> 00:11:24,018
doors were locked.

333
00:11:24,198 --> 00:11:26,475
And in the houses along the quiet

334
00:11:26,475 --> 00:11:29,581
streets, the television played the late news,

335
00:11:29,581 --> 00:11:31,582
and then the national anthem,

336
00:11:31,582 --> 00:11:32,824
and then the snow.

337
00:11:33,174 --> 00:11:35,589
Those small towns are still out there.

338
00:11:35,769 --> 00:11:37,912
But many of the stores on Main

339
00:11:37,912 --> 00:11:39,412
Street are empty now,

340
00:11:39,412 --> 00:11:41,626
and the diner closed years ago,

341
00:11:41,626 --> 00:11:44,269
and the movie theater became a church

342
00:11:44,269 --> 00:11:45,626
or an antique shop.

343
00:11:45,806 --> 00:11:48,185
The kids who rode their bicycles to

344
00:11:48,185 --> 00:11:49,953
the five-and-dime grew up,

345
00:11:49,953 --> 00:11:51,924
and a lot of them moved away.

346
00:11:52,274 --> 00:11:55,058
Maybe that's why those old color pictures

347
00:11:55,058 --> 00:11:55,873
hit so hard.

348
00:11:56,053 --> 00:11:57,414
In black and white,

349
00:11:57,414 --> 00:11:59,420
the past looks like history.

350
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:01,515
In color, it looks like a place

351
00:12:01,515 --> 00:12:03,060
you could walk back into.

352
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:04,552
The turquoise car.

353
00:12:04,732 --> 00:12:06,485
Your mother's yellow apron.

354
00:12:06,665 --> 00:12:09,010
The red stools at the soda fountain.

355
00:12:09,190 --> 00:12:10,293
It was real.

356
00:12:10,473 --> 00:12:11,669
And it was yours.

357
00:12:12,019 --> 00:12:14,016
So tell us in the comments.

358
00:12:14,196 --> 00:12:16,234
What was the name of your hometown,

359
00:12:16,234 --> 00:12:18,097
and what was on its main street?

360
00:12:18,277 --> 00:12:19,833
We read every comment.

361
00:12:20,013 --> 00:12:21,202
And stay with us,

362
00:12:21,202 --> 00:12:23,999
because next we're going to put nineteen

363
00:12:23,999 --> 00:12:26,377
sixty-two side by side with today,

364
00:12:26,377 --> 00:12:28,685
and ask who really had it better.

365
00:12:28,865 --> 00:12:30,659
Thank you for spending this time with

366
00:12:30,659 --> 00:12:30,804
me.
